{
  "id": "gke-cluster-access",
  "provider": "gcp",
  "title": "GKE: human access with Google identities, IAM and RBAC",
  "summary": "Users authenticate with their Google identity (gke-gcloud-auth-plugin). Access is the union of project-level IAM roles and Kubernetes RBAC, and Google Groups can be RBAC subjects.",
  "notes": [
    "IAM and RBAC are additive: a request is allowed if EITHER grants it. A broad IAM role such as roles/container.developer at project level reaches every namespace in every cluster in the project.",
    "For namespace-level access, keep IAM minimal (roles/container.clusterViewer to fetch credentials) and grant in-cluster permissions with RoleBindings.",
    "Google Groups for RBAC: groups must be nested in gke-security-groups@<your-domain>, and the cluster must have the feature enabled.",
    "Legacy client certificates and static passwords are disabled by default on modern clusters. Keep it that way."
  ],
  "docs": [
    {
      "title": "Authorize actions in clusters with RBAC",
      "url": "https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/role-based-access-control"
    },
    {
      "title": "Google Groups for RBAC",
      "url": "https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/google-groups-rbac"
    },
    {
      "title": "Create IAM policies for GKE",
      "url": "https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/iam"
    },
    {
      "title": "Install kubectl and configure cluster access",
      "url": "https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/cluster-access-for-kubectl"
    }
  ],
